Add [IEJoin](https://vldb.org/pvldb/vol8/p2074-khayyat.pdf) operator for faster performance of double range predicates of the form:

```sql
SELECT east.id, west.t id FROM east, west
WHERE east.dur < west.time AND east.rev > west.cost;
```
Benchmarking shows that the performance is 1-2 orders of magnitude faster than our existing operators (NestedLoopJoin and PiecewiseMergeJoin) for this type of query, especially when considering that practical use cases should not significantly increase the result set cardinality.

Some implementation notes:
* Only the "Union Arrays on Join Attributes" version from §4.2 was implemented, as the other variants are more complex and less space efficient;
* The paper describes incorrect handling of partial equality predicates, especially sort order and backtracking;
* The Bloom filter size has been empirically determined to be optimal at 1024:1 on an 18 core iMac Pro;
* The operator is a double pipeline breaker and the side being built in the `Sink` phases is tracked in the global state;
* Matching pairs and unmatched outer entries are emitted in the `Source` phase;
* The distributed version (Algorithm 3) is implemented by joining blocks on parallel threads;
* Additional predicates and outer joins are supported;
* "Simple" joins are not supported and are deferred to NLJ (this is future work);

Long story for small deletion.

I suggested this test as part of adding single string support.
- https://github.com/tj/commander.js/pull/1678#issuecomment-1047218753

I noticed it today because it is not covered by a unit test. Rather than adding a unit test, I think perhaps just delete the check. I don't think it is providing much value, and I suggest deleting it and if anyone reports problems using the API then add tests when we know what mistakes people make.

The previous situation was the routine expected an array and would silently do the wrong thing if a single string was passed. And two of us made that very mistake!

```
// early API required array like:
option.conflicts(['one']);
// early API silently did weird things if passed single string
option.conflicts('one');
```

New API supports both forms:
```
option.conflicts(['one']);
option.conflicts('one');
```
 
What I think the most likely user mistake now might be, and not too likely:
```
option.conflicts('one', 'two');
```

And the current test won't detect that! So suggesting keep it simple, delete for now.

 This PR is about **displaying the number of groups that have met the ending criteria for the group experiment**. We were not having the information of how many groups have actually completed the `ending-criteria` and how close is the experiment to being changed to `enrollment-complete` state.
 
In the ending criteria dialogue box, a line below displays the number of groups. This is only for the group experiments with ending criteria depending upon the number of groups.

 I created a group experiment with ending criteria as 2 groups and 2 participants per group.
 The first image displays the initial state of the experiment. As currently no participants have been involved, the group count is zero.
 In the second image, after involving two users with the below information, the group count is now 1. This is because in the school1 group, two participants have been enrolled.
```
"group": {
       "add-group1": ["school1"]
   },
   "workingGroup": {
       "add-group1": "school1"
    }
```
 In the third image, after involving two users with the below information, the group count is now 2. The experiment state also changed to `enrollment-complete` state.
```
"group": {
       "add-group1": ["school2"]
   },
   "workingGroup": {
       "add-group1": "school2"
    }
```
